00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:02.399 align:start position:0%
in the united states one in five  patients  hospitalized is re-hospitalized within  30 days of discharge  the question is why do some hospitals  have very low readmission rates relative  while other hospitals have much higher  readmission rates to investigate this  issue we looked at the diagnoses and  timing of hospital readmissions at the  highest performing hospitals in the u.s  with the lowest readmission rates  and compared them to the diagnoses and  timing of readmissions and the lowest  performing hospitals in the u.s with  much higher readmission  we essentially found that readmission  diagnoses and timing  are very similar regardless of hospital  performance  the implication here is that if you want  to reduce your readmissions at your own  hospital think about broad strategies  that can be used to reduce readmissions  across diagnostic categories and time  periods after discharge  rather than focusing on specific  diagnoses and time periods after  discharge because empirically in the  united states  the best performing hospitals have  reduced readmissions across the board
 

